.TH LLAPI_SCAN_CHUNK_PARSE 3 2026-11-04 "Lustre User API" "Lustre Library Functions"
.SH NAME
llapi_scan_chunk_parse, llapi_scan_chunk_inode, llapi_scan_chunk_skipped \- read LFU Object Stream chunks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <lustre/lustreapi.h>
.PP
.BI "int llapi_scan_chunk_parse(const void *" buf ", size_t " len ,
.BI "                           __u32 " chunk_size ,
.BI "                           struct llapi_scan_chunk *" ch ");"
.BI "int llapi_scan_chunk_inode(const struct llapi_scan_chunk *" ch ,
.BI "                           __u32 " idx ,
.BI "                           struct llapi_scan_inode *" li ");"
.BI "int llapi_scan_chunk_skipped(const struct llapi_scan_chunk *" ch ,
.BI "                             __u32 " idx ", struct lu_fid *" fid ,
.BI "                             int *" error ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The LFU Object Stream carries many objects at once. Its format is
defined in Cap'n Proto by
.IR lfu.capnp ,
installed beside the Lustre headers as
.IR <linux/lustre/lfu.capnp> ,
and
.I <linux/lustre/lustre_lfu_capnp.h>
gives its enum values and field offsets to C.
.LP
A stream is read as a bulk: an array of slots of
.I chunk_size
bytes, a power of two between
.B LFU_CHUNK_SIZE_MIN
and
.BR LFU_CHUNK_SIZE_MAX .
Each chunk is one unpacked, single-segment Cap'n Proto message whose root
is an
.BR LfuChunk .
It starts on a slot boundary and spans one slot, or several for a
"jumbo" chunk holding an object too large for one. These functions read
such chunks in place, without a Cap'n Proto library and without copying
them. Any other Cap'n Proto reader may read the same bytes.
.LP
.B llapi_scan_chunk_parse()
reads the chunk that starts at
.IR buf ,
which must be 8-byte aligned, with
.I len
bytes valid from there to the end of the bulk. It fills
.I ch
with the chunk's counters and the number of slots it spans,
.IR lc_slots ,
which is how far to step to the next chunk:
.LP
.nf
.RS
struct llapi_scan_chunk {
        __u32   lc_slots;       /* slots this chunk spans */
        __u32   lc_scanned;     /* LfuChunk.scanned */
        __u32   lc_errors;      /* LfuChunk.errors */
        __u32   lc_which;       /* enum lfu_chunk_which */
        __u32   lc_count;       /* records */
        __u32   lc_skipped;     /* entries of LfuChunk.skipped */
        struct llapi_scan_data lc_cursor;
        __u64   lc_private[6];  /* the reader's own */
};
.RE
.fi
.LP
.I lc_which
says which member of the chunk's union it carries.
.B LFU_CHUNK_INODES
holds
.I lc_count
records;
.B LFU_CHUNK_EMPTY
holds none and reports progress only. The other members are not decoded
here, and their
.I lc_count
is 0.
.I lc_cursor
is where a read resumes after this chunk.
.LP
.B llapi_scan_chunk_inode()
decodes record
.I idx
of an inodes chunk into
.IR li :
.LP
.nf
.RS
struct llapi_scan_data {
        const void      *ld_buf;        /* NULL: absent */
        __u32            ld_len;
        __u32            ld_padding;
};
\&
struct llapi_scan_inode {
        struct lu_fid   li_fid;
        __u64           li_size;
        __u64           li_blocks;
        __s64           li_atime;
        __s64           li_mtime;
        __s64           li_ctime;
        __s64           li_btime;
        __u32           li_mode;
        __u32           li_uid;
        __u32           li_gid;
        __u32           li_projid;
        __u32           li_nlink;
        __u32           li_flags;
        __u32           li_rdev;
        __u32           li_valid;       /* LFU_ATTR_BIT() */
        __u64           li_ino;
        __u32           li_atime_nsec;
        __u32           li_mtime_nsec;
        __u32           li_ctime_nsec;
        __u32           li_btime_nsec;
        __u64           li_spare[1];
        struct llapi_scan_data li_ea[LLAPI_SCAN_EA_SLOTS];
};
.RE
.fi
.LP
The scalars are in host order. The timestamps are seconds since the
epoch, and
.IR li_atime_nsec ,
.IR li_mtime_nsec ,
.I li_ctime_nsec
and
.I li_btime_nsec
the nanoseconds within those seconds, 0 where the source keeps whole
seconds; the
.B LFU_ATTR_*
bit of each time covers its nanoseconds.
.I li_ino
is the object's inode number on its target, as the backend numbers it:
not unique across targets, and not the number a client sees. It is 0
where the source has none.
.I li_flags
holds
.B LUSTRE_*_FL
inode flags.
.I li_valid
says which scalars are filled when the stream has
.BR LFU_FEATURE_PARTIAL_RECORDS ;
otherwise all of them are.
.I li_ea
is indexed by
.BR "enum lfu_ea" :
.BR LFU_EA_PATH ,
the path from the root of a namespace scan, then the values of
.BR trusted.fid ,
.BR trusted.link ,
.BR trusted.lov ,
.BR trusted.lmv ,
.BR trusted.hsm ,
.BR trusted.som ,
.BR trusted.lma ,
.BR trusted.dmv ,
and at
.B LFU_EA_ENC
the encryption context, as the target stores them. Only the Data fields,
those below
.BR LFU_EA_DATA_MAX ,
have a slot.
.BR LFU_EA_XATTRS ,
the list of other extended attributes, is not decoded and its slot stays
empty, so a record that carries one shows no sign of it. The pointers
point into the chunk and are valid as long as it is.
.LP
.I li_spare
and the slots of
.I li_ea
from
.B LFU_EA_DATA_MAX
on are room for fields a later version of the format adds, and are zero
until then. The whole structure is always filled, so a program built
against an older header keeps working with a newer library.
.LP
.B llapi_scan_chunk_skipped()
gives entry
.I idx
of the chunk's list of objects that a producer could not send: the
object's FID in
.I fid
and the negative errno that says why in
.IR error .
.LP
The bytes are not trusted. Every pointer is checked against the chunk
before it is followed, and a struct written by an older version of the
schema reads as zero past its end. A chunk from another process, a file
or another node can be read safely.
.SH RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
.B llapi_scan_chunk_parse()
returns
.B -EINVAL
for bytes that are not a chunk of this format: an unaligned
.IR buf ,
an invalid
.IR chunk_size ,
more than one segment, a segment that does not end on a slot boundary
within
.IR len ,
or a root whose pointers leave the chunk.
.B llapi_scan_chunk_inode()
returns
.B -EINVAL
for a record whose EAs point outside the chunk.
.B llapi_scan_chunk_inode()
and
.B llapi_scan_chunk_skipped()
return
.B -ERANGE
for an
.I idx
past the end of their list.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the FID of every object in a bulk of
.I nslots
slots:
.LP
.EX
        size_t off = 0;
\&
        while (off < (size_t)nslots * chunk_size) {
                struct llapi_scan_chunk ch;
                struct llapi_scan_inode li;
                __u32 i;
\&
                if (llapi_scan_chunk_parse(buf + off,
                                          nslots * chunk_size - off,
                                          chunk_size, &ch) != 0)
                        break;
                for (i = 0; i < ch.lc_count; i++)
                        if (llapi_scan_chunk_inode(&ch, i, &li) == 0)
                                printf(DFID"\\n", PFID(&li.li_fid));
                off += (size_t)ch.lc_slots * chunk_size;
        }
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
.BR llapi_scan_chunk_parse() ,
.B llapi_scan_chunk_inode()
and
.B llapi_scan_chunk_skipped()
are part of the
.BR lustre (7)
user application interface library since release 2.18.0.
.\" Added in commit v2_17_60-xxx-gxxxxxxxxx
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR llapi_scan_device (3),
.BR lustreapi (7)
